course: dictionary
lesson: 531
title: Clever words: astute
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Astute means clever in a sharp way. It is good at noticing things quickly and using them to your advantage. Look at the clue in this sentence: «'Stute» is a clipped word. A clipped word is a long word with a part cut off, so «'Stute» comes from «astute».
explain: The story tells you what astute means: «'Stute» is short for «astute», which means clever. In the Whale story, the Fish is called «'Stute» because it is clever, so the clipped word helps you see the meaning. Ask yourself: what kind of fish is clever enough to be called astute?
explain: The apostrophe matters. It shows where letters are missing, just as in «don't» and «it's». When you see an apostrophe at the start of a word, check whether the full word is missing its first letters. Then look up the full word, and check that your meaning fits the sentence.
fact: Astute is an adjective: it describes a person or thing that is clever.
fact: «Astute» means clever at understanding people and situations quickly, and using that to your advantage.
fact: An astute detective notices small clues that other people miss.
fact: An astute businessperson spots a good deal. «An astute observation» is a sharp, wise remark.
fact: A clipped word, like «'Stute» from «astute», has a part cut off, and the apostrophe shows the missing letters.
exercise: The Whale says the «'Stute Fish» was left. What does «'Stute» mean? | Slow and sleepy | Wet and cold | Very large | *Clever
exercise: An astute detective notices small clues. What does astute mean here? | Loud and noisy | *Quick to notice and understand | Rich and famous | Very tired
exercise: In «'Stute», what does the apostrophe show? | That the word is a question | *Where letters are missing | That the word is very long | That the word is a name
test: In the Just So Stories, the Cat is astute. Which word means nearly the same? | sleepy | hungry | loud | *clever
test: «An astute observation» is which of these? | A loud shout | A sleepy yawn | *A sharp, wise remark | A long list of things to buy
exam: Which sentence uses astute correctly? | The astute rain fell all afternoon. | She astute ate her lunch quickly and went out. | *The astute businessperson spotted a good deal before anyone else. | The astute pond was very cold and wet.
